-- title:
--   Proj base change for the projective Weierstrass model
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $V$ be a projective Weierstrass curve over $R$, i.e. a term of `WeierstrassCurve.Projective R`. Let $K$ be a field equipped with an $R$-algebra structure (in the same universe as $R$), and let $V.\mathrm{baseChange}\,K$ be the projective Weierstrass curve over $K$ obtained by applying $R \to K$ to the coefficients of $V$. For a projective Weierstrass curve, `projModelCR` denotes $\operatorname{Proj}$ of the grading `projModelGradingCR`, namely the grading induced on the quotient of $R[X_0,X_1,X_2]$ by the homogeneous ideal `projModelHomogeneousIdealCR` generated by the Weierstrass cubic, with graded pieces the images of the homogeneous submodules; and `projModelStrCR` denotes the structure morphism $\operatorname{Proj} \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ obtained as `Proj.toSpecZero` followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the algebra map from $R$ to the degree-zero part of the grading. The assertion is that there exists a morphism of schemes $\alpha \colon$ `projModelCR (V.baseChange K)` $\to$ `projModelCR V` making the square with sides $\alpha$, the structure morphism of the $K$-model to $\operatorname{Spec} K$, the structure morphism of the $R$-model to $\operatorname{Spec} R$, and $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to K$ a pullback square in the category of schemes; that is, `projModelCR (V.baseChange K)` together with these two morphisms realises the fibre product of the $R$-model with $\operatorname{Spec} K$ over $\operatorname{Spec} R$.
--
--   This is the statement that the projective (plane cubic) Weierstrass model commutes with base change, in the form of a universal pullback square rather than a mere isomorphism of schemes; the construction of $\alpha$ rests on the affine pushout descriptions of the graded ring map induced by coefficient extension on the charts away from $Y$ and away from $Z$. It is used to transport properness, integrality and reducedness of Weierstrass models along base change, and to produce the isomorphism between the base-changed model and the scheme-theoretic fibre product.
-- source:
